<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<task id="078" project="unified-access-control-r2">
  <metadata>
    <title>Authorize GET /api/v1/containers/{containerId}/documents — lists any container's documents behind RequireAuthorization() alone</title>
    <phase>0c Secure Documents — Wave 1 (added mid-wave)</phase>
    <status>completed</status>
    <rigor>FULL</rigor>
    <rigor-reason>An unauthenticated-by-resource read of document metadata keyed by a caller-supplied container id — the read-side twin of task 073's write-side hole.</rigor-reason>
    <model-tier>opus</model-tier>
    <model-tier-reason>Needs the container→record mapping decision to agree with tasks 073 and 075 rather than forking a third one.</model-tier-reason>
    <effort>high</effort>
    <effort-reason>Small surface; the judgement is in reusing the shared mapping rather than in the route itself.</effort-reason>
    <parallel-group>wave1</parallel-group>
    <parallel-safe>true</parallel-safe>
    <parallel-reason>Confined to the container documents endpoint plus read-only use of the shared container mapping.</parallel-reason>
    <deps>075</deps>
    <tags>bff-api, api, auth, security</tags>
    <estimated-effort>2-4 hours</estimated-effort>
    <origin>Found 2026-08-25 by task 074's route-authorization ArchTest on its first run — the SIXTH miss, present in no Wave 1 task and in neither the workflow review nor task 022's sweep.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>This route takes a container id from the URL and lists that container's documents, gated only by `RequireAuthorization()` — authentication, not authorization. Nothing checks that the caller has any relationship to the container or to the record that owns it.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE BFF security engineer.</role>

  <goal>A caller can only list documents in a container they are entitled to see, decided from the owning record. Unresolvable containers are refused.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>`GET /api/v1/containers/{containerId}/documents` returns document metadata for an arbitrary caller-supplied container id, behind `.RequireAuthorization()` and no per-resource filter.

This is the READ-side twin of task 073 (`PUT /api/containers/{containerId}/files/{*path}`, the write side). Both take a container id straight off the route and neither authorizes it against anything. Under the broker-only decision the per-project container is the blast-radius boundary; a route that enumerates any container's contents by id punctures that boundary from the read side, and — like task 073 — it does not depend on the caller holding an SPE container ACL, so it is not mitigated by the fact that nobody has one.

THE MAPPING IS SHARED, NOT NEW. Task 075 builds the record-aware container resolver, bidirectionally, and task 073 consumes the container→record direction. This task consumes the same mapping. There must be exactly ONE container→record mapping in the codebase; if implementing this appears to need a second, that is the signal to stop and reconcile with 075.

SEQUENCING. Depends on task 075 for the mapping. If 075 has not landed, do not build a private lookup — coordinate.</background>
    <relevant-files>
      <file role="modify">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Api/DataverseDocumentsEndpoints.cs</file>
      <!-- UploadEndpoints.cs was listed here as the reference for "the read-side twin of 073". Task 073
           DELETED that file on 2026-08-27 (it retired the three routes rather than gating them), so the
           reference is dead. The retirement REASONING is what mattered and it now lives in
           tests/integration/regression/MiContainerKeyedWriteRouteRetirementTests.cs. Read that instead —
           and note the asymmetry it implies for THIS task: 073 could delete because its routes had no
           legitimate caller; this one has callers, so it must be GATED. -->
      <file role="reference">tests/integration/regression/MiContainerKeyedWriteRouteRetirementTests.cs</file>
      <file role="reference">src/server/shared/Spaarke.Core/Auth/AuthorizationService.cs</file>
    </relevant-files>
  </context>

  <constraints>
    <constraint source="ADR-003">If the container cannot be mapped to an owning record, DENY. Never list "anyway".</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Consume task 075's mapping. Do NOT build a second container→record lookup.</constraint>
    <constraint source="build-plan">Do not solve this by granting the caller an SPE container permission. `GrantMembershipAsync` stays at zero callers.</constraint>
    <constraint source="project">Inventory client callers before tightening — a refusal that breaks a legitimate list view gets reverted, which reopens the hole.</constraint>
  </constraints>

  <knowledge>
    <files>
      <file>projects/unified-access-control-r2/SECURE-DOCUMENTS-BUILD-PLAN.md</file>
      <file>.claude/constraints/bff-extensions.md</file>
    </files>
  </knowledge>

  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Locate the route and confirm it still carries no per-resource filter. Inventory client callers with grep evidence.</step>
    <step order="1">Resolve container → owning record via task 075's mapping; authorize the caller's Read on that record.</step>
    <step order="2">Tests: a caller without Read on the owning record is refused; an unresolvable container is refused; legitimate list views still work.</step>
    <step order="3">Build, tests, publish size, CVE. Confirm task 074's rule no longer waives this route, and DELETE its waiver entry.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If containers legitimately exist that map to no record, STOP and surface it — same modelling gap task 073 names. Do not invent an owner.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`AuthorizationService` answers "may this caller act on this record"; task 075 supplies container→record.</existing>
    <extension>Adds the missing check to an existing route using an existing mapping. No new component.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>Any authenticated caller can enumerate the document metadata of any container by id, including a secure project's dedicated container — with no SPE ACL required, so nothing else stops it.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">The container documents endpoint, authorized against the owning record</output>
    <output type="test">tests/ — unauthorized-container listing denied; unresolvable container denied</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion>A caller without Read on the owning record cannot list that container's documents — proven by a test that fails without the change.</criterion>
    <criterion>An unresolvable container id is refused.</criterion>
    <criterion>Exactly one container→record mapping exists, shared with tasks 073 and 075.</criterion>
    <criterion>Legitimate client callers still work (inventory evidenced).</criterion>
    <criterion>Task 074's waiver for this route is deleted, not merely updated.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Found by the forcing function on its first run, in a project that had already enumerated this surface four times by hand. That is the argument for task 074 in one sentence.

COMPLETED 2026-08-28. Full write-up: notes/task-078-container-document-list-gate.md.

Gated by a new endpoint filter (ADR-008), `Api/Filters/ContainerDocumentAuthorizationFilter.cs`, attached at
`DataverseDocumentsEndpoints.cs:605`. Reuses THREE existing seams and adds none: task 075's
`RecordContainerResolver.ResolveOwningRecordAsync` (container->record, its first consumer),
`SemanticSearchAuthorizationFilter.TryResolveAuthorizableEntitySet` (the one entity-set map), and task 070's
`AuthorizationService.GetCallerRecordAccessAsync` (the rights decision, OBO as the caller). Concrete
`RecordContainerResolver` per ADR-010 — no interface added. Task 074's waiver DELETED, not converted.

TWO STALE CLAIMS IN THIS POML, both corrected in the notes rather than worked around:

(1) The relevant-files comment asserts "this one has callers, so it must be GATED". FALSE — the route has
    ZERO callers anywhere in src/, tests/ or scripts/ (inventory in notes §3). It was gated anyway, per
    instruction; the zero-caller fact is what makes the fail-closed refusal safe.

(2) The cost-of-doing-nothing claims any caller "can enumerate the document metadata of any container by
    id". The STRUCTURAL hole was real, but the data path is blocked by an unrelated pre-existing type bug:
    `sprk_containerid` is NVARCHAR holding an SPE `b!...` id, while the route does `Guid.TryParse` and the
    service does `Guid.Parse` (notes §4). So the route currently 400s or returns empty for every input.
    Gating first is still the correct order — the type bug is one line from being fixed.

ESCALATION TRIGGER FIRED (notes §2). Containers that map to no record DO legitimately exist — every shared
business-unit / archive container; verified live per RecordContainerResolver.cs:61 and :267. This task
REFUSES them (ADR-003, and this POML's own acceptance criterion) and did NOT invent an owner. That
contradicts task 075's stated assumption at RecordContainerResolver.cs:415-425 that 078 would ALLOW on null
("breaking task 078 for every normal container"); the zero-caller inventory is what makes refusing safe.
SUCCESSOR OBLIGATION: listing a SHARED container's documents needs Wave 3 result trimming
(AccessibleRecordSetService), not a relaxation of this gate.

STEP 9.5 REVIEW FOUND ONE REAL DEFECT (notes §5), now fixed: the denial was a four-way ORACLE. A distinct
errorCode per branch plus the resolver's propagating 409 ("More than one record claims this container") let
an unauthorized caller partition container ids by ownership state BEFORE any rights check. Collapsed to one
`Denied(correlationId)` — one status, one detail, one code — with the resolver's SdapProblemException caught
and folded in; new guard AllResourceSideRefusals_AreIndistinguishableToTheCaller. Also fixed: missing bearer
token now answers 401 before the resolver's Dataverse round trips (was a 403 mislabel); and a FALSE claim in
my own comment ("task 073 consumed the forward direction" — git log shows 075 delivered its own forward
consumer; 073 consumed neither direction).

Perturbation-checked FIVE ways (notes §6), all bit: rights check disabled; allow-on-null; per-branch error
code re-introduced; resolver 409 allowed to propagate; filter removed -> ArchTest Rule A fails naming the
route. All restored.

Verification: build 0 warnings; 7/7 task tests; 41/41 with resolver neighbours; 10/10 RouteAuthorizationGuardTests;
ArchTests 121 passed / 6 failed == clean-tree baseline (proven by git stash); publish 45.12 MB compressed
incl. PDBs (+0.16 vs 44.96 baseline, ceiling 60); no vulnerable packages; no packages added.
</task>
